(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).asep=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep.full||[]).push({
  num: 11, part: `Part Three: Integration and Beyond`, title: `Test Like You Fly`, weeks: `Weeks 28 to 33`,
  tasks: [`T9`,`T11`,`M3`,`M8`,`O4`,`X1`,`X3`,`X5`],
  cast: [
    [`Tess Okonjo`,`Commander. A government astronaut on Halo's first crew. The person the requirements are for.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Brannock's email came in at ten to five on Friday. The long cure lot had passed its fifteen year aging test. Nadia read it, sent it on to Wes and Odette, and turned back to the harness on Tiago's desk.`,
    `The rack's flight power harness was supposed to be in the rack. The stand-in's, made of cargo wire, was there instead, and had been for nine days. The as-built list said otherwise. The proof test started Tuesday.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Monday it goes back in,"</span> Tiago said. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Six o'clock. I'll be here."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Tonight it goes on the list."</span>`,
    `She wrote the discrepancy report at his desk with her coat still on: what was installed, since when, how it got there, and that the rack had run a week of checks on a harness built for a box of ballast. At twenty to six the as-built list said so. Putting the flight harness back became a numbered step, with a check after it and two names beside it.`,
    `Tiago read it over her shoulder. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"It's Friday night,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"It could've waited till Monday."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"That's how it got to nine days."</span>`,
    `Gus was in at six on Monday. Ines stood at the rack bay with the report on a clipboard while he made the swap, and she read the flight harness's label out loud before it went in. Then Tiago ran the power stage again from the top, ten percent, half, full, as if the rack had never been switched on. Every reading came up inside the band Jun had written down for it.`,
    `Wes read Brannock's email at the coffee urn with his glasses down his nose. He folded them and put them in his shirt pocket.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Good,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Now let's see the rest of it hold air."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Colleen's Flight Notes: test like you fly`,
    storyAt: 3, // Story only: her Saturday of week twenty nine plays before f11s4 (week thirty)
    sections: [
      { h: `Bench and sky`,
        body: [`<!--story-->Colleen's new radio had passed every check on the shop's bench at Erie. On Saturday of week twenty nine she asked Nadia if she wanted to ride along on the check flight, and an hour later Nadia was in the right seat of the blue and white airplane at six thousand five hundred feet over Barr Lake, in a borrowed headset that pinched.`,
          `<!--story--><span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Dry, light, pleased.">"The shop proved it does what the box says,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Dry, light, pleased.">"Up here I find out whether I can hear Denver approach with it."</span>`,
          `Verification asks whether the system was built right. It gives objective evidence that an element or a system meets its specified requirements, and it happens at every level: each element against its own requirements, each subsystem against its, the whole system against the system requirements. Validation asks whether it's the right system. It gives objective evidence that the system, in use, meets the stakeholders' needs and does the job it was meant for, in the environment it was meant for. The strongest validation evidence comes from real users in realistic conditions. Validation isn't saved for the end, either: needs, concepts of operation, requirements and models can all be checked against what stakeholders really need along the way. A system can pass every verification and still fail validation, and when it does, something between the need and the evidence lost the need.`],
        terms: [[`Verification`,`Objective evidence that a system or element meets its specified requirements. Built right.`],[`Validation`,`Objective evidence that the system, in use, meets stakeholder needs in its intended environment. Right system.`]],
        exam: `Verification: against requirements, at each level. Validation: against stakeholder needs, in intended use and environment, ideally with real users. Passing one doesn't prove the other.` },
      { h: `The test card`,
        body: [`<!--story-->Clipped to her knee was a card in her small handwriting, one line per check: what, how, what counts as good, at what altitude and power. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"If I can't say what good looks like before I take off,"</span> she said, <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"I'm sightseeing."</span>`,
          `Each requirement gets a verification method: inspection, analysis, demonstration or test, plus analogy or similarity, or sampling, where those are justified. The verification matrix records, for every requirement, the method, the level where it's verified, the event or test that does it, the success criteria, the evidence, and its status. Verification is planned when requirements are written, which is one reason a requirement has to be verifiable. The process then defines procedures and the enabling systems the work needs, performs the verification, compares results with the criteria, records them, and reports anything that doesn't conform. A requirement closes when its evidence has been reviewed against its criteria and accepted, and the matrix shows that status to everyone who needs it.`],
        terms: [[`Verification matrix`,`A table recording each requirement's method, level, event, success criteria, evidence and status.`],[`Requirement closure`,`Accepting objective evidence, reviewed against the success criteria, that a requirement has been met.`]],
        exam: `For every requirement: method, level, event, criteria, evidence, status. Closure takes objective evidence reviewed against criteria, not a test having been run.` },
      { h: `Strobes`,
        body: [`<!--story-->Over the lake she switched the strobe lights on. The radio buzzed with every flash. She switched them off and the buzz went away. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Quick, pleased, exact.">"Strobe power supply,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Quick, pleased, exact.">"The bench never had both of them on at once."</span>`,
          `Test like you fly means testing in the configuration, environment and way the system will really be used. Where that isn't possible, know exactly what the test leaves out and cover it some other way. Pressure vessels get a proof test above their maximum design pressure to show the structure holds with margin and to screen out workmanship flaws, then a leak test at working pressure, because loading past working pressure can open a leak path. Thermal vacuum testing runs hardware in vacuum through its hot and cold extremes, with cycling, while it operates. Vibration and acoustic tests stand in for launch. Electromagnetic interference testing, EMI, checks what each box emits and how well it tolerates what's around it. Electromagnetic compatibility belongs to the whole system, where harnesses, grounds and every box meet, so it's verified at that level too. Qualification levels prove a design with margin. Acceptance levels screen each flight unit for workmanship without using up its life. Protoflight testing puts a flight unit through qualification levels for acceptance durations when there's no separate qualification unit.`],
        terms: [[`Test like you fly`,`Test in the real configuration, environment and use; where you can't, name the gap and cover it another way.`],[`Electromagnetic compatibility (EMC)`,`A system's ability to work in its electromagnetic environment without causing or suffering interference.`]],
        exam: `Proof before leak. Thermal vacuum: vacuum plus temperature, while operating. EMC is a system property, verified at system level. Qualification proves the design; acceptance screens each unit.` },
      { h: `Knock it off`,
        body: [`<!--story-->Back on the ground she showed Nadia the top line of the card, boxed: the conditions that would end the flight on the spot, agreed before the engine started. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Quiet, firm, kind.">"You decide what stops you while you're still calm,"</span> she said.`,
          `A test readiness review comes before a formal test. It confirms the test article's configuration is known and controlled, procedures are approved, facilities and instruments are ready and calibrated, people are trained, hazards to people and hardware are controlled, success criteria are agreed, and every open item has been dispositioned: which ones stop the test and which can proceed as actions. Its outcome is a decision, made on stated criteria, to proceed, proceed with actions, or hold.`,
          `Quality assurance works on the project. It gives independent assurance that processes and products follow their plans, procedures and requirements: witnessing key steps, checking records and calibration, handling nonconformances, and driving root cause and corrective action, then checking the action worked. It isn't the same as quality management, the organizational process that sets the policy and system QA applies. It isn't verification either, though it watches verification closely.`],
        terms: [[`Test readiness review (TRR)`,`A review confirming article, procedures, facilities, people, safety and open items are ready before a formal test.`],[`Quality assurance`,`Independent assurance, on a project, that processes and products conform to plans and requirements.`]],
        exam: `TRR: ready to test, on stated criteria, with open items dispositioned. QA: independent, project level, witness and records, nonconformance, root cause, corrective action.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f11s1`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T9`, title:`Six hundred and twelve rows`, q:`Every row has a method, and Halo's verification lead still turned the laptop around. What should Nadia do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p10=(all&&all[10]&&all[10].picks)||{};const noconf=G&&G.noconfigcapture;return [`Monday afternoon Halo's verification lead flew in for Jun's matrix.`+(p10[11]===3?` Ines and Jun had spent the morning walking the module against the as-built list and drawings, and had found two labels turned to the wall and nothing else.`:``)+(noconf?` Friday's report had caught the data harness too. The list had called it flight since Thursday, over the stand-in's, and its shield drawing went to the board that afternoon with the power harness step.`:``),
        `The matrix had six hundred and twelve rows, one for every requirement, derived ones included, and every row had a method. She scrolled for ten minutes, then turned the laptop around. Forty three rows said test and nothing else. No level. No event. Nothing that said what would count as a pass.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"They say test,"</span> Amara said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Tiago's going to test them. What's missing?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Give each of the forty three its level, an event and the criteria for a pass.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun and Halo's lead take the forty three row by row. Each gets the level where it's verified, the test that does it, a number that counts as a pass, and a place for the evidence. Nine turn out to have no test planned at all. Three were going to be closed on box tests standing in for the whole module. By Wednesday every row in the matrix reads the same way, and Halo's lead stops scrolling.`,
          why:`A verification matrix records, for each requirement, the method, level, event, success criteria and evidence. Without them a row can be closed by a test that ran, not by evidence that the requirement was met.`},
        {t:`Add pass criteria now, and fill in levels and events as each test is booked.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The criteria go in. The levels wait, and two rows are still planned as box tests for module requirements when Tiago books the chamber.`,
          why:`Criteria matter most, but the level and event are part of the plan too. A test at the wrong level can meet its criteria and still not verify the requirement.`},
        {t:`Leave the rows as they are, with the criteria held in Tiago's procedures.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Halo's lead asks for the procedures. Nine of the forty three don't have one yet.`,
          why:`The matrix is where verification is planned and tracked. If the criteria live only in procedures, no one can see what's missing until the test is due.`},
        {t:`Switch the forty three to analysis, for Jun to close from the model this week.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`Halo's lead asks why forty three tests just became analyses on a Monday. Amara can't say, and the rows go back to test.`,
          why:`The method follows what evidence the requirement needs, agreed with the customer. Changing methods to close rows faster weakens the evidence without a reason.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s2`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M8`, title:`Witness`, q:`Ines has marked steps in the proof procedure where quality will watch. What should Nadia decide?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday morning Ines brought the proof procedure to the cell marked in green: five steps where quality would stand and watch before anyone went on. The calibration stickers on every gauge and transducer. Both plugs going in. The pressure hold. The strain readings. The closeout.`,`Tiago read her five steps and shrugged.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Ines standing there adds a day,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"I've run every proof this cell's ever seen."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Ines review the full data package in the days after the test is done.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The package is clean when she reads it. Whether the plugs went in the way the photos say, she has to take on faith.`,
          why:`Reviewing records helps, but some things can only be confirmed while they happen. Witness points put independent eyes on those steps.`},
        {t:`Let Tiago sign for quality as the person who knows that cell best.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Tiago signs his own work, and does it well. OCSC's observer asks who checked it, and the answer is the same name.`,
          why:`Quality assurance has to be independent of the people doing the work. Expertise doesn't replace independence.`},
        {t:`Keep Ines at her five witness points with her signature on each.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia keeps all five of Ines's witness points. At the first one Ines checks every sticker against the calibration lab's list. One gauge on the fill panel isn't on it. It's the cell's old spare, and it gets swapped before a valve opens. She watches both plugs go in and initials each one. The test loses forty minutes, not a day.`,
          why:`Quality assurance gives independent assurance that work follows its plans and that the evidence is objective. Witnessing key steps, checking calibration and signing apart from the doers are core QA.`},
        {t:`Count Halo's witness at the cell as the quality check for the proof.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Halo's witness watches the gauges. Nobody from FRO checks the stickers, and one gauge is due.`,
          why:`A customer witness is oversight for acceptance. The supplier still owns its own quality assurance.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s3`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T9`, title:`Plugs`, q:`The leak test read a third of allowable, with ground plugs in both relief ports. When should it close?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The nitrogen ran into HEARTH all Tuesday night. Proof came first on Wednesday morning, because pushing a shell past its working pressure is what opens a leak if one is waiting, and the leak test is there to find it afterward. Wes stood in the cell until Tiago sent him out, then stood at the window with his palm flat on the glass.`,`The pressure went up in steps and held. The strain gauges climbed, held, and came back to zero on the way down. Then twenty four hours at working pressure, corrected for the cell's temperature. Thursday afternoon Jun put the number up. A third of the allowable leak rate.`,`Both relief ports still had the ground plugs in them, long red handles and all. The two flight relief valves sat on Tiago's bench with their bench test tags.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Close it,"</span> Wes said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Those plugs seal tighter than any valve."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Close the module's leak row now, with a new row for the relief valves alone.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The valves get their row. The module's row says closed on a configuration that won't fly, and nothing ties the two together.`,
          why:`Testing the difference is right, but the requirement is for the flight configuration. Its closure should rest on evidence that covers that configuration.`},
        {t:`Close it on Thursday's number, with the plugs noted as tighter than valves.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The requirement closes. Two valve flanges that have never held cabin pressure in place are covered by a test that had plugs in them.`,
          why:`A test verifies the configuration that was tested. When the article differs from flight, the difference needs its own evidence.`},
        {t:`Close it with the valves bolted on, on the strength of their bench tests.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The valves go on. Their bench tests said nothing about the flange joints they were just bolted into.`,
          why:`A bench test covers the valve, not its installation. The joint made at installation is part of the flight configuration.`},
        {t:`Close it once the valves are back on and leak checked right where they sit.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia keeps the row open until the valves are checked where they sit. They go back on Friday. Gus adds a trace of helium to the fill and checks each flange with a sniffer while the module holds pressure, and both come in clean. The closure record names Thursday's run, the plugs that were in for it, and Friday's two checks, so the evidence covers exactly what will fly.`,
          why:`Verification evidence counts for the configuration tested. When the test article differs from flight, the difference is recorded and covered by another check, so the flight configuration itself is verified.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s4`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T9`, title:`Cold side`, q:`In Longmont, the flight forward hatch sits in a rented thermal vacuum chamber. How should the test run?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p8=(all&&all[8]&&all[8].picks)||{};const cut=G&&G.cuttest;return [`In week thirty the flight forward hatch rode up the highway to Longmont on a flatbed, wrapped like furniture, to a thermal vacuum chamber FRO had rented for a week. Its seals came from Brannock's long cure lot. Tiago's fixture held cabin pressure on one side, as HEARTH would, and vacuum on the other. Wes rode up in the truck.`,
        (cut
          ?`No hatch of this design had ever been in a chamber. The qualification article had gone to storage with no leak test behind it, so the first one measured would be the one that flew. Tiago had written this run at protoflight levels: qualification temperatures, acceptance durations. There was no qualification number to hold it against. This one would have to be it.`
          :p8[0]===1
            ?`The qualification hatch was still waiting its turn for a leak test. Nothing of this design had been cold in vacuum yet.`
            :`The qualification hatch had held air at room temperature in week eighteen. Nothing of this design had been cold in vacuum yet.`),
        `Amara had the chamber's day rate on her phone. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"The seal just passed fifteen years of aging,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Do we need two days of cold soak on top of that?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Drop the cold soak, with the aging result standing in for it.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The hatch sees hot and ambient. Whether a stiff seal holds at the cold end of its orbit, nobody will know until it's there.`,
          why:`Aging data shows how a material changes over years. It doesn't show how the assembly performs at temperature in vacuum.`},
        {t:`Measure the leak hot and cold, working the latches at each plateau.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`At the cold plateau the leak rate climbs to about two thirds of the allowable, then settles back as the hatch warms. The latches take more force cold, still inside what a crew can pull. Every number goes in the record. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Cold rubber's stiff,"</span> Wes says, reading it. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Now we know how stiff."</span>`,
          why:`Thermal vacuum testing verifies function and performance in vacuum across the temperature extremes, while operating. Test like you fly: measure and operate the hardware the way it will be used, at the conditions it will see.`},
        {t:`Run the cycles in the chamber, then measure leak once at room temperature.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The hatch comes out tight. What the seal did at the cold end, while it was cold, wasn't measured.`,
          why:`Cycling screens workmanship, but performance has to be measured at the extremes, not only after them.`},
        {t:`Cycle it in FRO's own oven in air, with the chamber days given back.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The oven cycles it. With air around it, the hatch never sees vacuum on one side and cabin pressure on the other.`,
          why:`Vacuum changes heat transfer and loads. Thermal testing in air leaves out the environment the hardware will fly in.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s5`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T9`, title:`Green`, q:`Saturday's EMI run passed the power distribution unit in every band. What should that result close?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On Friday of week thirty her own row closed. Tiago held a calibrated smoke source under the cabin sensor in the main air return above the galley, and the alarm went at four seconds, with sixty allowed. Ines read the record and signed the closure. Fire detection, verified by test. Nadia felt the small click, and this time she didn't argue with it.`,`Saturday she drove to Colorado Springs. FRO had rented a day in her old shop's EMI chamber, the electromagnetic interference room where boxes get tested for electrical noise, for HEARTH's power distribution unit, and Beto Salinas, her old bench partner, was running it from the same chair, with the same bad coffee. The box passed emissions and susceptibility in every band. He saw her laptop open to the matrix and leaned over.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Beto" data-tone="Low, warm, pleased.">"Look at that. Green."</span> He grinned. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"You still love a green PASS, Brooks."</span> She told him her own row had closed the day before, fire detection, four seconds. He clinked his bad coffee against hers. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"Bench is the easy part,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"Call me when somebody's slept in it."</span>`,`Then her phone went. Amara, from home. Halo wanted the module's electromagnetic compatibility requirement, EMC, closed on today's result. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Noisiest box aboard just passed,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Can I tell them it's done?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Close the module's EMC row on this box's result, the noisiest one aboard.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The row closes. The rack, the fans, the harnesses and the grounds have never been tested together for it.`,
          why:`A requirement is verified at the level where it's specified. One box's results can't show how the whole module behaves.`},
        {t:`Close the module's EMC row by analysis, adding up the results of every box's test.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Jun adds them up and it closes on paper. The analysis has no way to see a loop that only exists once the boxes share a ground.`,
          why:`Analysis can support a system requirement, but EMC depends on how elements interact through harnesses and grounds. That takes evidence at system level.`},
        {t:`Close the box's rows today, with the module's EMC row left for a module test.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The box's rows close that afternoon. The module's EMC row goes into the integrated test, with every box, the rack, the fans and every harness running, and the station radio frequencies watched. Beto hears about the ground loop from week twenty seven and laughs. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"That's the one,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"Box tests never see that one."</span>`,
          why:`Requirements are verified at the level where they're specified. Element results support a system requirement like EMC, but closing it takes evidence from the integrated system, where the interactions live.`},
        {t:`Close it on integration, where HEARTH ran all week without a network drop.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`No drops is a good sign. It isn't a measured emission, and it says nothing about the bands the station's radios use.`,
          why:`Normal operation isn't a test with criteria. Verification needs planned measurements against the requirement.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s6`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T11`, title:`Who sleeps there`, q:`Halo is sending Commander Tess Okonjo and three crew for the evaluation. Who should run the scenarios, and how?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const skip=G&&G.skippedconops;return [`Monday of week thirty one Helen called. Halo was sending Commander Tess Okonjo and three of her crew on Wednesday for the human-in-the-loop evaluation: thirty six hours living in HEARTH on the high bay floor, then the emergency runs, ending Thursday night with the sleep period fire Helen had written in herself.`+(skip?` The sleep period scenarios had come late, after the requirements, the same way the operations concept had.`:``),`Halo's verification lead was at Nadia's desk with the matrix open on her laptop when the call ended.`,`Six hundred rows, she said, almost all closed or booked. She wanted to know what a crew was going to tell them that the matrix couldn't.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Halo's trainers run the scenarios, with the crew watching from outside.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Helen says the trainers are welcome, and the crew is coming anyway. She isn't signing anything a crew hasn't lived in.`,
          why:`Trainers help, but the strongest validation comes from the real users doing the real tasks in realistic conditions.`},
        {t:`Count the verification results as validation, with each requirement traced to a need.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Helen asks which requirement says a crew can sleep there. Her crew arrives Wednesday regardless.`,
          why:`Verification shows requirements were met. Validation shows the needs were met in use. Traceability doesn't turn one into the other.`},
        {t:`Have FRO's engineers run the scenarios, as the people who know the module.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The engineers run them fast because they know where everything is. Helen's crew arrives Wednesday and runs them again.`,
          why:`People who designed the system aren't representative users. Validation needs the people who will operate it.`},
        {t:`Have Halo's crew live in it and run every scenario from their own bunks at night.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Tess's crew will come in flight clothes. They'll eat, sleep and work in HEARTH for thirty six hours with the module's real procedures, then run every emergency scenario from their own bunks. Jun writes down what Halo needs to see from each run, need by need, before the first one starts.`,
          why:`Validation shows the system meets stakeholder needs in its intended use and environment. The strongest evidence comes from representative users doing real tasks in realistic conditions.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s7`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T11`, title:`Bunk four`, q:`Tess hands Nadia twenty seven items from a day and a half in HEARTH. What should happen to them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tess Okonjo came in Wednesday morning with a duffel bag and three crew, and asked to walk the module before anyone briefed her. Nadia took her down the aisle. Past the galley, Tess stopped between quarters three and four and looked at both.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Tess">"Which one would you take?"</span>`,`Nadia knew the numbers. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"One. By the hatch."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Tess">"Good answer."</span> Tess dropped her duffel into four, the far quarter. <span class="said" data-who="Tess">"Last one out is the commander's."</span> She took a photo out of her jacket, two boys on a dock holding one small fish between them, and taped it to the wall beside the pillow. <span class="said" data-who="Tess">"They like to know where I sleep."</span>`,`They lived in HEARTH for a day and a half. Thursday morning Tess handed Nadia a clipboard with twenty seven items on it in pencil. The galley table blocked the aisle when it was down. The hatch handle's cover caught a glove. The hygiene station's door swung out into the path. None of them failed a requirement. Amara read the list over Nadia's shoulder.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Every one of these meets spec,"</span> she said.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fix the ones Tess ranked highest, with the rest put on the sustaining list.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Tess's top five get fixed. The galley table isn't one of them, because she didn't know it would be in the escape path at night.`,
          why:`Prioritizing helps, but each finding should be traced to the need it touches and resolved with the stakeholder, not ranked by one reviewer alone.`},
        {t:`Close them as out of scope, with HEARTH meeting every requirement.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The list goes in a folder. The galley table still drops across the aisle on the first night of the first mission.`,
          why:`Validation findings are evidence about stakeholder needs. Meeting requirements doesn't make them out of scope.`},
        {t:`Trace each to the need it touches, then settle it with Halo's crew office.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun and Helen sit with Tess's list all afternoon. Each item gets the need it touches and how much it matters. Four become design changes, nine become procedure changes, and fourteen are accepted as they are, with a reason Tess signs. The galley table gets a latch that won't let it stay down across the aisle.`,
          why:`Validation findings are recorded, traced to the stakeholder needs they affect, and resolved with the stakeholder: a change, a procedure, or accepted with rationale.`},
        {t:`Fix all twenty seven before ship, with nothing left on the crew's list.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Amara prices it at three weeks. Half the fixes are for things Tess said she'd rather keep.`,
          why:`Not every finding needs a change. Each is weighed against the need with the stakeholder, and some are accepted.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s8`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Technical`, task:`T11`, title:`Sleep period`, q:`The tracer pooled in quarter four while the cabin sensor barely moved. What should Nadia do now?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday at ten the lights went out in HEARTH. Four crew in four bunks, curtains drawn. Odette had the stop button. Helen sat at Tiago's console in a headset, watching four screens, one particle counter for each quarter, hung where the crew's heads would be because she had asked for them there. In the aft hatch a duct fan stood in for the air Halo's node would push through on orbit. Behind the hygiene station's panel Tiago's generator waited to put out tracer smoke where a fan motor might burn.`,`It started at fourteen past. The cabin sensor would alarm, the crew would wake, and Jun's stopwatch would run from there.`,`The counters in one, two and three stayed flat. Four climbed. The cabin sensor's trace gave one small bump, nowhere near its alarm, and went flat again. At three minutes quarter four's counter crossed the level that would have set the sensor off. Odette looked over at Helen. Helen shook her head. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"It's tracer. I want to see if it finds her."</span> At five it was twice that. Odette's hand was over the button.`,`At six minutes four's curtain moved. <span class="said" data-who="Tess">"Tiago,"</span> Tess said from the dark, in a perfectly ordinary voice. <span class="said" data-who="Tess">"Is this supposed to be in here?"</span> Odette stopped the run.`,`Somebody hit the work lights. Nadia went down the aisle. Tess was sitting up on the edge of bunk four in her socks with the stuff still all around her, gray, from the ceiling down to a hand off the pillow. It smelled sweet, like the fog machine at a high school dance. In the aisle you could see where the duct fan pulled: clean air, galley to aft hatch, one straight shot. Four's opening sat half behind the hygiene wall, out of it. With the curtain shut, the air just went on by. Her boys were still taped up by the pillow, them and their one fish, gone soft in the gray.`,`Helen had got as far as the galley, headset still on, the cord swinging where she'd pulled it out of the console. She was looking at the curtain. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"I asked for the curtains,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"So they'd hear the alarm."</span>`,`Amara had come down behind Helen. She looked at the pillow, and put her phone back in her pocket.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"The sensor passed."</span> It was out before Nadia could stop it. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I closed that row. Four seconds."</span>`,`Tiago had the camera up. He let it down, and he didn't say it unkindly. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"At the detector,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Nobody sleeps at the detector."</span>`,`Ernie at the switch with his tester, saying dead on the radio. Her under the galley vent watching Tiago's smoke can, typing PASS. Dad had been a mile down the road. Tess had been down the aisle with her boys on the wall.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rerun it tomorrow night with the same setup, then write it up for Halo.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It does the same thing Friday night. Helen had already seen it the first time, from the galley.`,
          why:`Repeating a result has value, but a validation failure is recorded and traced as soon as it's seen, with the evidence kept as it was.`},
        {t:`Move the cabin sensor toward quarter four tonight, then run it again.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Tiago won't touch the module until the run's been recorded. A sensor moved toward four would still be in the aisle.`,
          why:`Jumping to a fix before recording and understanding the failure risks fixing the wrong thing and losing the evidence.`},
        {t:`Leave the module as it stands, then trace the failure back to its start.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`No one touches the curtain. Tiago photographs the quarter and saves every counter's data. That night Nadia and Jun trace it back. The requirement said detect smoke anywhere in the habitable volume within sixty seconds. The verification point said the cabin sensor. The hazard report's detection control cited that sensor's test. The requirement had been right. The place she had proved it hadn't.`,
          why:`A validation failure means the system doesn't meet the need in real use even though its requirements were recorded as verified. Keep the evidence, record it, and trace back through requirements, verification and hazard controls to find where the need was lost.`},
        {t:`Record it as outside the requirement, which the cabin sensor met.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Helen asks to see the requirement. It says anywhere in the habitable volume, and quarter four is in it.`,
          why:`A verified requirement doesn't make a failure in use out of scope. Validation exists to catch exactly this.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s9`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M8`, title:`What kind of problem`, q:`Jun's model now shows the curtain pocket even with gravity off. How should the finding be handled?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She called her sister Callie from the car in the lot at a quarter to one. Callie was on nights. She picked up on the second ring, and the first thing she said was <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Who's hurt?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"No one. No one's hurt."</span> Then she told it, badly and out of order. The tracer. The counter. Quarter four. The photo on the wall. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I gave the call, Cal. I said dead, and they went to bed under it."</span>`,`The line was quiet long enough that Nadia looked at the screen to see if she'd lost her.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Did anybody climb?"</span> Callie asked.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"No. She found it. The commander. She sleeps in it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Then your crew tested where they touch,"</span> Callie said. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"That's the whole thing. It's never one call, and yours was one call."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"You said that at the sink."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"I said a lot of things at the sink."</span>`,`A radio went off behind her, somebody wanting a switching order. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Hang on."</span> She keyed it. Nadia listened to her sister read the order out, then sit through the crew lead reading the whole thing back, then ask him where he was going to test it, and wait till he said. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Go ahead."</span> She came back on. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Sorry."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Don't be. That's the first of three. You don't give Ernie's call. I knew that at the sink, and I said it anyway. I'm sorry."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Well, I'm sorry about twice a year,"</span> Callie said. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"It's one in the morning and you called me first, so I'm counting it."</span> The radio again. She let it go a second. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"You were right too. One check is one check. I just hate it when you're the one saying it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Somebody did leave one in, a few weeks back. Nine days."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"And somebody went and looked."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Thank you."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Don't make it a thing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"I'm not telling Dad till you've had your big review. Go to bed."</span>`,`Friday Jun ran the smoke model again with the curtain in it. The assumption page from the trade still said what it had said then: the cabin modeled as one open volume, fans at nominal. The curtain had never been in it. With one, the model put a pocket in quarter four inside three minutes. He ran it again with gravity off, as on orbit, where nothing rises and only the fans move smoke. The pocket was still there.`,`Ines came up from quality at ten with the drawings under her arm. <span class="said" data-who="Ines">"Every part in that module matches its drawing,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ines">"So tell me what kind of problem this is."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Change the design through the board, and have Ines find why verification missed it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The change request puts a detector in every quarter at the head of the bunk, the station's standard unit. Halo has four in its spares and can ship them in week thirty four. Ines opens a corrective action on FRO's verification planning. The root cause is hers to write: verification points for hazard controls were chosen for how testable they were, and no one checked them against where the crew would be in each scenario. Now every one gets that check. She reruns it on all forty hazard reports, and two more points move. She'll check the next program's matrix herself.`,
          why:`Every part matches its drawing, so a nonconformance against the hardware has nothing to disposition. The gap is in the design and the verification plan, so the design changes through change control, and QA drives root cause and corrective action on the process that let it through, then checks that the fix worked.`},
        {t:`Change the design through the board, verify the change, and close the finding.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The detectors go in. The way FRO picks verification points stays the same, and so does whatever else it picked that way.`,
          why:`Fixing the design is necessary. QA also asks why the process allowed the gap, and corrects the process.`},
        {t:`Write a nonconformance against the cabin sensor and send it to the review board.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The review board finds the sensor built and installed exactly to its drawing, and sends the report back.`,
          why:`A nonconformance is a product that departs from its requirements or design. This sensor didn't. The gap is in the design and the verification plan.`},
        {t:`Close it as no defect, with the hardware built and installed to every drawing.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Ines won't close it. A module that matches its drawings and lets smoke pool over a pillow is a quality problem, just not a hardware one.`,
          why:`Quality assurance covers processes as well as products. A process that produced a conforming but unsafe design needs corrective action.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s10`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`, task:`O4`, title:`On the line`, q:`Jun's verification status review is Monday at nine, and Nadia's week is full. What role should she take?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Jun's verification status review with Halo and OCSC, the federal office that audits human rating, was Monday at nine: three hours, every open row. Every review Jun had given so far, Nadia had been in the room for.`,`Friday afternoon Amara stopped at her desk. Nadia had the detector change, the hazard report and Odette for the whole week ahead. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Do you want to take Monday yourself,"</span> Amara asked, <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"or push it?"</span>`,`Jun was two desks over and heard it. He didn't look up. Later, at the urn, he asked her straight out. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Will you be on the line?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let Jun run it alone, with what he can commit to agreed first.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia tells Jun the review is his. They spend twenty minutes Friday on what he can agree to in the room and what comes back to her. Monday she's in quarter four with Odette and a tape measure, her phone in her desk drawer. OCSC's observer asks to close thirty rows on cargo heritage. Jun takes them one at a time, agrees to eleven where the conditions match, and says no to nineteen with reasons. Afterward he finds her at quarter four. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"I'd bring up the fire row myself next time,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"I waited to be asked."</span> He'd said no nineteen times, and he doesn't mention it.`,
          why:`Developing people means real responsibility with clear limits, room to do the work without being rescued, and reflection after. Competency grows through practice, not observation.`},
        {t:`Let Jun run it, with her dialed in on mute, ready to step in if needed.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She doesn't step in once. Jun can see her name on the call list the whole three hours, and answers every question looking at it.`,
          why:`A safety net is kind, but a visible one keeps the junior person leaning on it. Agreed limits and a debrief build more.`},
        {t:`Run it herself this time, with Jun taking notes beside her as before.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The review goes well. Jun takes notes for her, as he has at every review so far.`,
          why:`Taking back the work in a hard week is understandable, but it costs the junior person the experience that builds competency.`},
        {t:`Push the review a week for her to sit beside him one more time.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Halo's verification lead can't move it. Jun runs it Monday anyway, without the preparation they'd have done.`,
          why:`Waiting for perfect conditions delays growth. Prepare people and let them lead.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s11`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M3`, title:`Pie`, q:`Twenty three open items sit on the TRR list. What should Nadia do with them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const hid=G&&G.hidrisk;return [`Thursday night Colleen met her at the diner on Arapahoe Road and ordered pie for both of them without asking. She listened to all of it: quarter four, the photo, what Tiago said about the detector. She didn't say any of the things Nadia had braced for. She cut a corner off her pie and left it on the plate.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Plain, level, remembering.">"I was a captain,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Plain, level, remembering.">"Night before a deployment move, a jet came out of maintenance, and on the engine run it felt wrong. Not a number. A feel, through the seat. Every gauge in the green. My colonel wanted it on the ramp by morning, and he asked me if I had anything besides a feeling. I didn't. So I flew it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"And?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Flat, plain, no pride in it.">"It came back. Everybody shook my hand."</span> She turned her coffee cup a little. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Level, honest, unhurried.">"Coming back isn't the same as being right. I've known that a long time. I never found out if anything was wrong with that jet. What I know is I had a room where I could have said no, and I said yes, because I couldn't prove it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I can prove mine."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Warm, plain, firm.">"Then you've got it easier than I did,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Warm, plain, firm.">"You still have to say it."</span> She picked up the check. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"I told you something in a booth like this once. About gates."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I remember."</span>`,`Friday morning Amara put the open items for the test readiness review, the TRR, on the HEARTH room screen. Twenty three of them, from the detector change to a procedure redline nobody had signed. The integrated acceptance test started Monday afternoon if the review said so.`+(hid?` The board's chair would be at the TRR. She had asked to be, after the seal.`:``),`<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Which of these stop Monday?"</span> Amara asked.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Hold the test until all twenty three are closed, with not one going in open.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Amara won't hold six hundred rows of testing for a torn label and a late set of meeting notes. She writes her own criteria over the weekend and sorts them herself.`,
          why:`Holding for everything is safe but not a decision on criteria. Items that can't affect safety or results can proceed as actions.`},
        {t:`Take all twenty three in as open items, to be closed while the test runs.`, s:0, flag:`rushedtrr`, d:{trust:4,conf:-3,health:-6},
          after:`The list goes to the TRR as twenty three open actions, none blocking. The schedule holds on paper.`,
          why:`Going into a formal test with unresolved items that affect safety, configuration or results can waste the test or put people and hardware at risk.`},
        {t:`Ask each item's owner whether theirs blocks, then sort on their answers.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`No owner says theirs blocks. Amara sorts them again over the weekend, with criteria written at the top.`,
          why:`Owners know their items, but without shared criteria every judgment is different. Decisions need stated criteria.`},
        {t:`Write the blocking criteria first, then sort all twenty three against them.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia writes the blocking criteria at the top of the list before anyone sorts: could it hurt someone, change what's being tested, or spoil a result. Amara's name and Odette's go under them. Four items meet one: the detectors that aren't in yet, a software build not yet released, the unsigned redline, and one transducer past its calibration date. The other nineteen go in as actions, each with an owner and a date. Every call gets one line of reason.`,
          why:`Decision management sets the criteria before judging the options, so the decision is consistent and on record. A TRR decides on stated criteria which open items block the test and which can proceed as actions.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s12`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X5`, title:`Her row`, q:`After the Sunday call to Pueblo, Nadia opens the TRR package. What should she do about her row?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday at eight she called Pueblo. Callie put Rosie on, and Rosie held the rocket up to the camera with its green door open until her arm got tired. Their father was out back in the tomatoes, Callie said, and gave her a look that said that was on purpose.`,`Then Nadia opened the TRR package. Jun had built it from the matrix. The fire detection row had read the same since Friday of week thirty: verified by test, closed, PASS, N. Brooks. The detector change sat in its own section as a finding from the crew evaluation, with Odette's hazard report beside it. Nothing in the package said who had picked the cabin sensor, or why.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Report it as a test finding with the fix and dates, leaving out whose call it was.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Every fact in it is true. Anyone reading it would think the gap had come from nowhere.`,
          why:`Accurate reporting is the floor. Ethical practice also means owning one's part, so others can judge the rest of one's work.`},
        {t:`Reopen her own row in red, with her name and her reasoning written under it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`She writes one page. The row goes red and says reopened. Under it: the point she chose in the architecture work, why she chose it, what it missed in quarter four, and every other row she verified the same way, which she's checking herself before Monday. She sends it to Jun and Amara at eleven, so neither of them sees it first in the room.`,
          why:`SE ethics asks for honesty and accountability: disclose your own errors promptly to the people relying on your work, and own the correction.`},
        {t:`Leave the row closed, and show the detectors as an addition to the design.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`The row stays green. The requirement it claims to have verified was never met in quarter four.`,
          why:`Leaving a known false closure on the record misleads everyone who relies on it, including the crew.`},
        {t:`Put it down to the smoke model, which had one volume and no curtain.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`It's true the model left the curtain out. It's also true the model didn't pick the verification point.`,
          why:`Pointing to a contributing cause to avoid owning a decision is a failure of accountability, even when the cause is real.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f11s7`, title:`Verification or validation?`, intro:`Six things that happened in HEARTH's test weeks. Pick what each one is.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Tiago holds a calibrated smoke source under a sensor and times the alarm.`, choices:[`Verification by test`,`Verification by inspection`,`Validation`,`Verification by analysis`], answer:0, why:`A measured result under controlled conditions against a criterion is verification by test.`},
      {prompt:`Ines reads the part number and stamp on each relief valve before it goes on.`, choices:[`Verification by test`,`Verification by inspection`,`Validation`,`Verification by demonstration`], answer:1, why:`Examining an item for marks or features is verification by inspection.`},
      {prompt:`Jun uses the structural model to show the shell lasts fifteen years.`, choices:[`Validation`,`Verification by demonstration`,`Verification by analysis`,`Verification by test`], answer:2, why:`A life that can't be tested in full is shown with models and calculation: verification by analysis.`},
      {prompt:`Tess's crew lives in HEARTH for a day and a half and lists what gets in their way.`, choices:[`Verification by demonstration`,`Verification by test`,`Verification by inspection`,`Validation`], answer:3, why:`Real users in realistic use, judged against their needs, is validation.`},
      {prompt:`The hatch is opened and latched once at each temperature, and it works or it doesn't.`, choices:[`Verification by demonstration`,`Verification by analysis`,`Validation`,`Verification by inspection`], answer:0, why:`Observed operation, judged pass or fail without detailed measurement, is verification by demonstration.`},
      {prompt:`The module's leak rate is measured over a day at working pressure.`, choices:[`Verification by inspection`,`Verification by test`,`Validation`,`Verification by demonstration`], answer:1, why:`A measured value under controlled conditions against a limit is verification by test.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`What is the main purpose of the verification process?`, opts:[`Give objective evidence that an element or system meets its requirements`,`Show the finished system meets stakeholder needs when it is used as intended`,`Combine realized elements into a whole and check each interface as it's made`,`Confirm the organization's processes are followed on every project it runs`], a:0, why:`Verification provides objective evidence that a system or element meets its specified requirements. Meeting needs in use is validation, combining elements is integration, and process conformance is quality assurance.`, task:`T9`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Why is a pressure vessel's leak test usually run after its proof test?`, opts:[`A proof test needs the vessel already sealed tight to reach pressure`,`The leak test's gas fill is reused for proof, which saves a full day`,`Proof loading can open leak paths, and the leak test should catch them`,`Proof testing calibrates the gauges that the leak test relies on later`], a:2, why:`Proof loads the structure above its working pressure, which can open a leak path. Running the leak test afterward measures the vessel as it will fly.`, task:`T9`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A unit passed every functional test at room temperature in air. What does thermal vacuum testing add?`, opts:[`Proof that it was built to its drawings, part by part`,`Its function and performance in vacuum at the extremes`,`How well it holds up to the shaking and noise of launch`,`Evidence that users can run it under real workloads`], a:1, why:`Thermal vacuum testing runs hardware in vacuum through its hot and cold extremes while it operates. Drawings are inspection, launch is vibration and acoustic, and users are validation.`, task:`T9`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Each box in a module passes its own EMI tests. The module also has a system level EMC requirement. How should that requirement be closed?`, opts:[`On the box results, since every source of noise was tested`,`By analysis alone, adding each box's emissions together`,`By the customer's approval of each box's test report`,`On a test of the integrated module with everything running`], a:3, why:`Requirements are verified at the level where they're specified. EMC depends on how boxes, harnesses and grounds interact, so it needs evidence from the integrated system.`, task:`T9`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`What is the main purpose of the validation process?`, opts:[`Show the system meets its specified requirements at each design level`,`Show the system, in use, meets stakeholder needs in its environment`,`Show each element was built to its design, with records of how`,`Show the system has moved to its users, with the support they need`], a:1, why:`Validation gives objective evidence that the system, in use, meets stakeholder needs in its intended environment. Requirements are verification, building elements is implementation, and handover is transition.`, task:`T11`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Who usually gives the strongest validation evidence for a crewed habitat?`, opts:[`The designers, since they know how each part is meant to work`,`An independent analyst who reviews the verification reports`,`Representative crew using it in realistic operating conditions`,`The supplier's quality group, signing off each finished element`], a:2, why:`Validation is strongest with representative users doing real tasks in realistic conditions. Designers aren't representative, and reports and sign-offs are verification and assurance.`, task:`T11`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Every requirement on a system has been verified, but a validation run shows it doesn't meet a stakeholder need. What does this most likely mean?`, opts:[`The validation run was flawed and should be repeated until it passes`,`The stakeholder's need changed after the requirements were set`,`The system is acceptable, since the contract measures verification`,`The need was lost somewhere in requirements, verification or models`], a:3, why:`When verification passes and validation fails, something between the need and the evidence lost the need: a requirement, a verification choice or a model. Trace back to find it.`, task:`T11`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`What is the main purpose of the quality assurance process on a project?`, opts:[`Independent assurance that products and processes meet plans and requirements`,`Setting the organization's quality policy and objectives for every project`,`Measuring each product's performance against its technical targets over time`,`Running the verification tests that close the system's requirements one by one`], a:0, why:`Quality assurance gives independent assurance, on the project, that processes and products conform. Policy is quality management, performance tracking is measurement, and testing is verification.`, task:`M8`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`A module matches every drawing, yet a crew evaluation shows a hazard the design missed. Beyond fixing the design, what should quality assurance drive?`, opts:[`A nonconformance report against the hardware as it was built`,`A new inspection of every part against its released drawing`,`A waiver, since the hardware conforms to its approved design`,`Root cause and corrective action on the process, then a check`], a:3, why:`The hardware matches its drawings, so there's no hardware nonconformance. QA drives root cause and corrective action on the process that allowed the gap, and later checks the action worked.`, task:`M8`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`An engineer learns that test data she reported to a customer last month came from the wrong unit. What should she do?`, opts:[`Tell the customer promptly, correct the record, and own it`,`Correct it quietly in the next scheduled data delivery`,`Tell her manager privately, and leave the decision there`,`Wait for the next formal review to raise it with everyone`], a:0, why:`Ethical practice means honesty and accountability: disclose errors promptly to the people relying on the information, correct the record, and own the correction.`, task:`X5`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A junior engineer is ready to lead a customer review for the first time. What best develops his competency?`, opts:[`His lead sits beside him and takes the hardest questions`,`He leads alone, with limits agreed before and a debrief after`,`He watches several more reviews before he leads one himself`,`He leads, while his lead listens on mute to step in if needed`], a:1, why:`Competency grows through real responsibility with clear limits, room to do the work without rescue, and reflection afterward.`, task:`X5`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`What is a test readiness review mainly for?`, opts:[`Deciding whether the system is ready to ship to its customer`,`Confirming the design is mature enough to start fabrication`,`Checking the article, procedures, facility and people are ready`,`Approving the final test results and closing the requirements`], a:2, why:`A TRR confirms readiness for a formal test: article configuration, procedures, facilities and instruments, people, safety, criteria and open items. Shipping, design maturity and closure belong to other reviews.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Curtains were added so a sleeping crew member would hear the alarm. They also made a corner where smoke collects. What does this best illustrate?`, opts:[`A requirement that was never written to be singular`,`An emergent effect of parts and people interacting`,`A supplier that missed one detail of an interface`,`A test that relied on instruments out of calibration`], a:1, why:`Systems thinking: behavior that comes from parts, environment and people acting together, not from any one part. The curtain solved one need and created a new hazard.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`A team must decide which open items should stop a test from starting. What should come first?`, opts:[`Agree the criteria an item has to meet to block the test`,`Rank the items by how long each one will take to close`,`Ask the customer which items it wants closed beforehand`,`Close the quickest items, and then judge the ones left`], a:0, why:`Decision management defines the criteria before judging the options, so the decision is consistent, defensible and recorded.`, task:`M3`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`A smoke model treated a cabin as one open volume. A test then found smoke pooling behind a curtain the model left out. What's the best response?`, opts:[`Keep the model as it is, since one test can be an outlier`,`Stop using models for smoke, and rely on testing alone`,`Add the curtain, and check the model against the test data`,`Tune the model's numbers until its output matches the test`], a:2, why:`Models are checked against reality. When a test shows a missing feature, add it, compare the model with the test data again, and record the assumption change. Tuning numbers hides the gap.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{G=G||{};const pk=s.picks||{};const rushed=(s.flags&&s.flags.rushedtrr)||G.rushedtrr;const hid=G.hidrisk;
    return [
    `The TRR started at eight on Monday of week thirty three in the big conference room off the lobby, the one with the lighting company's old logo still etched in the glass. Helen and Ken Mallory, Halo's program manager. The OCSC observer. Ines, Odette, Wes, Tiago, and Amara in the chair.`+(hid?` The board's chair sat beside the observer.`:``)+` Colleen came in late and took a seat by the wall.`,
    `Jun walked the matrix.`+(pk[9]===0?` He did it as he had the Monday before, and didn't look at Nadia once.`:``)+` Six hundred and twelve rows.`+(rushed
      ?` Twenty three open items, all of them going into the test open, to be closed while it ran. That was how she had sorted them.`
      :pk[10]===3
        ?` Twenty three open items: four that held their part of the test, nineteen going in as actions with owners and dates.`
        :` Twenty three open items, sorted again by Amara over the weekend against three lines she'd written at the top.`),
    `Then the fire detection row came up on the screen.`+(pk[11]===1
      ?` It said reopened, in red, with her name in the owner column and her page under it.`
      :pk[11]===2
        ?` It still said closed, in green. Verified by test.`
        :pk[11]===3
          ?` Under it was her line about the smoke model's assumption, and nothing else.`
          :` It said what the package said: a finding from the crew evaluation, the detectors, the dates. Nothing about whose call it had been.`),
    `Nadia stood up.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I picked the cabin sensor as the verification point for fire detection,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Back when we built the architecture. I picked it because all the cabin air comes back there, and because it was easy to test, and because I knew it would pass. It passed. In the crew run Commander Okonjo lay in quarter four with smoke a hand off her pillow, and it never went off."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"The requirement was right. Detect smoke anywhere in the habitable volume. I verified it in one place, the place it would pass. That gap is mine. Not the model's, and not the curtain's."</span>`,
    `She looked at the back wall. Colleen hadn't moved.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"A gate is where you're allowed to say we're not ready,"</span> Nadia said. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Fire detection isn't ready. It said ready from week thirty on, because I signed it.`+(pk[11]!==1?` Reopen it, and hold`:` Hold`)+` its part of the test until there's a detector in every quarter and we've proved each one at the pillow, curtain shut, from every source in every scenario."</span>`,
    `Amara didn't poll the room. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Proceed on everything else,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Fire detection holds, and the fire hazard report reopens with it. Nadia owns the verification. Odette owns the hazard."</span> She wrote it into the minutes herself, and for once she didn't ask what it would cost.`,
    `Helen caught Nadia at the door afterward. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"Tess asked me to tell you something,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"She's keeping four."</span>`,
    `Her phone rang at seven that night while she was still in the lot. Dad, the screen said. He never called on a Monday.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Your sister says you two are talking,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Says you had a couple of weeks."</span>`,
    `She told him. All of it, in order this time, as she'd told the room. The tracer. Quarter four. The photo. Tiago. The sensor she'd picked because it would pass. He let her get all the way to the end.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I did Ernie's job,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Tested it at the switch and called it dead."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Ernie checked it right,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Sounds like you did too. That curtain wasn't on you, any more than that fella's generator was on him."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Don't."</span> It came out harder than she meant. <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"You can give Ernie that. He didn't pick the switch. I picked the sensor, Dad. I picked it because it would pass."</span>`,
    `There was only the traffic on Arapahoe for a while, through the windshield.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Okay,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Then it's yours."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"It's mine,"</span> she said, and since they were there anyway, she asked him for the other half. Why he'd never told her. He'd given her half of it out in the tomatoes, on one knee, and she'd driven home with it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Other half's worse,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Every class I ever had, I was the fella who climbed it. A room full of guys every year looking at my hand and knowing why. With you I was just your dad who got hurt."</span> She heard him breathe out. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"I liked being that. I wasn't in any hurry to trade it."</span>`,
    `She didn't have an answer for that, and he didn't seem to want one. Somebody on Arapahoe laid on a horn for no reason she could see. Then he asked about the picture. Where'd the commander put it. By her pillow, Nadia said. Scotch tape. Crooked.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Leave it up when you test it,"</span> he said.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch11.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Hearth`
});
